// 07 · the gradient — annotations are inputs, not obligations
//
// Every annotation in Mentl is optional and expressive. Leave one off and the
// program is still valid; add one and you UNLOCK something — a capability, a
// freedom, a sharper proof. The annotations form a partial order, and at any
// cursor the medium can name the next-most-useful one to add. That ranking is
// the gradient, and the cursor is its argmax: the position where adding one
// thing buys the most.
//
// This is the file to open in the medium, not only to read. Serve the IDE, put
// the cursor on a signature, and the aspect ring's Teach facet names the one
// annotation worth adding here — proven, because the medium tried it, verified
// it, and rolled it back before offering it. A `??` is you asking directly; it
// names a hole the medium proposes into.
//
// Edit it:  mentl space   →   http://localhost:7378/ide/   (paste this in)
// Read it:  mentl lib/tutorial/07-gradient.mn:23   ← the Teach facet names the next move
import io

// add carries no annotation. Inference still proves its row is Pure; the
// gradient here would offer `with Pure` — same body, and now the medium may
// memoize it, fold it at compile time, or run it beside anything.
fn add(a, b) = a + b

// The same body, the annotation taken. Nothing about the computation changed;
// what changed is what the medium is now allowed to do with it.
fn add_pure(a, b) with Pure = a + b

// A bigger unlock lives here. step allocates nothing, so `with !Alloc` makes it
// safe inside a real-time callback. The annotation did not make it faster; it
// stated a promise the medium could already prove, and unlocked the callers
// that need it. Put the cursor on it and the medium says the body proves more
// than you promised — Pure — and that tightening is the next move it offers.
fn step(x) with !Alloc = x * 2 - 1

fn main() = add_pure(step(20), 3) |> int_to_str |> print_string
